GRUB 2.14 on a UEFI with the Memory Attribute Protocol maps a module's
code read-only (grub_dl_set_mem_attrs). Its relocator keeps the registers
it hands the kernel inside that code (relocator64.S: grub_relocator64_rax,
_rip, _rsp), and the x86 linux loader -- the path for a kernel without an
EFI stub -- writes them there before it boots: the firmware stops it with
"X64 Exception Type - 0E(#PF)", W:1 P:1, the moment an entry boots. Code
sections stay writable; data keeps no-execute and read-only data stays
read-only.

--- a/grub-core/kern/dl.c
+++ b/grub-core/kern/dl.c
@@ -691,6 +691,13 @@
 	{
 	  set_attrs |= GRUB_MEM_ATTR_X;
 	  clear_attrs &= ~GRUB_MEM_ATTR_X;
+	  /* The relocator keeps the registers it hands the kernel in its own
+	     code (relocator64.S: grub_relocator64_rax, _rip, _rsp), and the x86
+	     linux loader writes them there before it boots a kernel without an
+	     EFI stub. Read-only code turns that write into a page fault in the
+	     firmware, on any UEFI that has the Memory Attribute Protocol. */
+	  set_attrs |= GRUB_MEM_ATTR_W;
+	  clear_attrs &= ~GRUB_MEM_ATTR_W;
 	}
 
       err = grub_update_mem_attrs ((grub_addr_t) seg->addr, seg->size,
